HTTP/1.1 (RFC 7230, 2014 — the modernised restatement of RFC 2616 from 1999, still dominant) is a text protocol : every request and response is human-readable ASCII headers followed by an optional body. Here's what a minimal checkout request actually looks like on the wire: And the response: Headers are key-value pairs separated by 
, with a blank line (

) dividing headers from body. The parser is simple — almost too simple, which is part of HTTP/1.1's problem. Keep-alive (persistent connections, on by default in HTTP/1.1) means one TCP connection can serve multiple requests sequentially, avoiding repeated 3-way handshakes. But "sequentially" is the operative word.
